Rule V1 (doc/scope_model.mld, section 3.5), with assumption D9 (section 2):
dune compiles every implementation of a virtual library against the one
interface of each virtual module. A driver documents that interface once in
the virtual library, and once more in each implementation, as an
implementation copy with the same name and digest. A copy is told apart from
the virtual library's unit by the implementation of that module next to it
(D9). When the candidates for a root have one name and one digest, V1 picks
the copy if exactly one candidate is a copy, and otherwise the virtual
library's unit if exactly one candidate is not a copy. In any other case the
root is unresolved.

The setup is what dune builds for a virtual library [vlib] with one virtual
module [Clock], and two implementations [impl_a] and [impl_b]:

[vlib] has dune's alias module [Vlib], with [module Clock = Vlib__Clock].
The file [vlib/vlib.ml] stands for dune's [vlib.ml-gen]. [clock.mli] is
compiled to [vlib__Clock.cmi] with [-open Vlib].

Each implementation has its own alias module, such as [Vlib__impl_a__], and
a [clock.ml] compiled against [vlib]'s [vlib__Clock.cmi], which is copied
into the implementation's directory: [-intf-suffix .ml] makes the compiler
use that [.cmi] and not write a new one. [impl_a] also has a private module
[Extra] compiled with [-open Vlib__impl_a__]. dune links [vlib]'s other
[.cmi] files into each implementation's directory; here [-I vlib] does the
same job.

[user] is built against [vlib] only. [tool] is built against [impl_a], and
so [vlib].

Every compile uses dune's [-no-alias-deps], and [-L] for the unit's own
library and its dependencies.

  $ cat impl_a/vlib__impl_a__.ml
  (* generated by dune *)
  
  (** @canonical Vlib.Clock *)
  module Clock = Vlib__Clock
  
  (** @canonical Vlib.Extra *)
  module Extra = Vlib__impl_a__Extra
  $ cat impl_a/clock.ml
  type t = int
  
  let now () = 1
  $ cat impl_b/clock.ml
  type t = float
  
  let now () = 2.0
  $ cat user/user.mli
  val stamp : unit -> Vlib.Clock.t
  (** See {!Vlib.Clock} and {!Vlib__Clock}. *)
  
  val direct : Vlib__Clock.t
  $ cat tool/tool.mli
  val stamp : unit -> Vlib.Clock.t
  
  val direct : Vlib__Clock.t
  $ cat vlib/vlib.ml
  (* generated by dune *)
  
  (** @canonical Vlib.Clock *)
  module Clock = Vlib__Clock
  $ cat vlib/clock.mli
  type t
  
  val now : unit -> t
  $ cat impl_a/extra.mli
  val direct : Vlib__Clock.t
  $ ocamlc -bin-annot -no-alias-deps -opaque -nopervasives -nostdlib -w -49 -I vlib -o vlib/vlib.cmo -c -impl vlib/vlib.ml
  $ ocamlc -bin-annot -no-alias-deps -opaque -I vlib -open Vlib -o vlib/vlib__Clock.cmi -c -intf vlib/clock.mli
  $ for i in a b; do
  >   ocamlc -bin-annot -no-alias-deps -opaque -nopervasives -nostdlib -w -49 -I impl_$i -o impl_$i/vlib__impl_${i}__.cmo -c -impl impl_$i/vlib__impl_${i}__.ml
  >   cp vlib/vlib__Clock.cmi impl_$i/
  >   ocamlc -bin-annot -no-alias-deps -opaque -I impl_$i -I vlib -intf-suffix .ml -open Vlib__impl_${i}__ -o impl_$i/vlib__Clock.cmo -c -impl impl_$i/clock.ml
  > done
  $ ocamlc -bin-annot -no-alias-deps -opaque -I impl_a -I vlib -open Vlib__impl_a__ -o impl_a/vlib__impl_a__Extra.cmi -c -intf impl_a/extra.mli
  $ ocamlc -bin-annot -no-alias-deps -I user -I vlib -c user/user.mli
  $ ocamlc -bin-annot -no-alias-deps -I tool -I impl_a -I vlib -c tool/tool.mli

The driver documents [vlib]'s [vlib__Clock.cmti] three times: once for
[vlib], and once for each implementation, with that implementation's
[--parent-id] and [-L] set. It also runs [odoc compile-impl] on each
implementation's [clock.ml], which puts that implementation next to the
copy (D9). This is what odoc_driver does: [Packages.remap_virtual] and
[Odoc_unit.fix_virtual] make the copies, and [Odoc_units_of.of_impl] runs
[odoc compile-impl] when it finds the source. dune also links [vlib]'s
[vlib.cmi] into each implementation's directory, with no [.cmt]; odoc_driver
does not document it again, as D9 says.

  $ odoc compile --output-dir h --parent-id pkg/vlib -L vlib:h/pkg/vlib vlib/vlib__Clock.cmti
  $ odoc compile --output-dir h --parent-id pkg/vlib -L vlib:h/pkg/vlib vlib/vlib.cmt
  $ for i in a b; do
  >   odoc compile --output-dir h --parent-id pkg/impl_$i -L impl_$i:h/pkg/impl_$i -L vlib:h/pkg/vlib vlib/vlib__Clock.cmti
  >   odoc compile-impl --output-dir h --parent-id pkg/impl_$i --source-id pkg/src/impl_$i/clock.ml -L impl_$i:h/pkg/impl_$i -L vlib:h/pkg/vlib impl_$i/vlib__Clock.cmt
  > done
  $ for i in a b; do
  >   odoc compile --output-dir h --parent-id pkg/impl_$i -L impl_$i:h/pkg/impl_$i -L vlib:h/pkg/vlib impl_$i/vlib__impl_${i}__.cmt
  > done
  $ odoc compile --output-dir h --parent-id pkg/impl_a -L impl_a:h/pkg/impl_a -L vlib:h/pkg/vlib impl_a/vlib__impl_a__Extra.cmti
  $ odoc compile --output-dir h --parent-id pkg/user -L user:h/pkg/user -L vlib:h/pkg/vlib user/user.cmti
  $ odoc compile --output-dir h --parent-id pkg/tool -L tool:h/pkg/tool -L impl_a:h/pkg/impl_a -L vlib:h/pkg/vlib tool/tool.cmti

How to read [odoc_print --short --long-paths --show-root-parents]. Each line
is one item of the signature. [x/0] is the item [x], with a number odoc adds
to tell apart items of the same name. [resolved(...)] is a resolved path, and
[root(pkg/vlib/Vlib)] is the unit [Vlib] of the library [vlib] in the
package [pkg]. [hidden(...)] marks a hidden unit, such as [Vlib__Clock],
which is never linked to (S3). [canonical(p,c)] is the path [p], shown as
its canonical target [c] (S1); [c] comes from a [@canonical] tag, and is
resolved at link. [root(/vlib?/Vlib)] is the root [Vlib] of such a tag
before link, looked up in [vlib] first (S2's known limit).

Case 1 (V1). A unit built against the virtual library only.

[user]'s [stamp] has the type [Vlib.Clock.t]. Its root [Vlib] should mean
[vlib]'s [Vlib], the only unit [Vlib]. [direct] names [Vlib__Clock] itself,
with the origin [user]. [user]'s libraries hold no copy, so it should mean
[vlib]'s unit too.

The link also resolves [user]'s comment, whose references are the subject of
case 5; any warning it prints comes from them.

  $ odoc link -L vlib:h/pkg/vlib -L impl_a:h/pkg/impl_a -L impl_b:h/pkg/impl_b -L user:h/pkg/user -L tool:h/pkg/tool h/pkg/user/user.odoc
  File "Vlib__Clock":
  Ambiguous lookup. Possible units: pkg/impl_a/Vlib__Clock, pkg/impl_b/Vlib__Clock, pkg/vlib/Vlib__Clock
  $ odoc_print --short --long-paths --show-root-parents h/pkg/user/user.odocl
  val stamp/0 : resolved(unit) -> resolved(canonical(root(pkg/vlib/Vlib).Clock,resolved(root(pkg/vlib/Vlib).Clock)).t)
  val direct/1 : resolved(hidden(root(pkg/vlib/Vlib__Clock)).t)

Case 2 (V1). A unit built against one implementation.

[tool]'s libraries are [tool], [impl_a] and [vlib]. [stamp]'s root [Vlib]
should mean [vlib]'s [Vlib], as in case 1. [direct]'s root has the origin
[tool]. Its candidates are [vlib]'s unit and [impl_a]'s copy, with one
digest. Only [impl_a]'s library holds an implementation of [Clock], so V1
should pick [impl_a]'s copy, at compile (the test compiles the
implementation first), and the link should keep it.

odoc does not tell a copy from the virtual library's unit yet (section 11):
at compile it takes the unit of the last [-L] that holds one. [direct]
meaning [vlib]'s unit would be wrong.

  $ odoc_print --short --long-paths --show-root-parents h/pkg/tool/tool.odoc
  val stamp/0 : resolved(unit) -> resolved(canonical(root(pkg/vlib/Vlib).Clock,root(/vlib?/Vlib).Clock).t)
  val direct/1 : resolved(hidden(root(pkg/vlib/Vlib__Clock)).t)
  $ odoc link -L vlib:h/pkg/vlib -L impl_a:h/pkg/impl_a -L impl_b:h/pkg/impl_b -L user:h/pkg/user -L tool:h/pkg/tool h/pkg/tool/tool.odoc
  $ odoc_print --short --long-paths --show-root-parents h/pkg/tool/tool.odocl
  val stamp/0 : resolved(unit) -> resolved(canonical(root(pkg/vlib/Vlib).Clock,resolved(root(pkg/vlib/Vlib).Clock)).t)
  val direct/1 : resolved(hidden(root(pkg/vlib/Vlib__Clock)).t)

Case 3 (V1). A unit of the implementation itself.

[impl_a]'s [Extra] names [Vlib__Clock] directly, so the root has the origin
[Extra]. Its candidates are [vlib]'s unit and [impl_a]'s copy, as for
[tool], so it should mean [impl_a]'s copy. [Extra] is hidden, so
odoc_driver does not link it, and the result at compile is the one that
stays.

[direct] meaning [vlib]'s unit would be wrong.

  $ odoc_print --short --long-paths --show-root-parents h/pkg/impl_a/vlib__impl_a__Extra.odoc
  val direct/0 : resolved(hidden(root(pkg/vlib/Vlib__Clock)).t)

Case 4 (V1). The order of the [-L] flags.

The answer should not depend on the order of the [-L] flags. Here [tool] and
[Extra] are compiled again, with [vlib] given before [impl_a]. [direct]
should still mean [impl_a]'s copy in both.

A result that changes with the [-L] order would be wrong, whichever
candidate it picks.

  $ odoc compile --output-dir h --parent-id pkg/tool -L tool:h/pkg/tool -L vlib:h/pkg/vlib -L impl_a:h/pkg/impl_a tool/tool.cmti
  $ odoc_print --short --long-paths --show-root-parents h/pkg/tool/tool.odoc
  val stamp/0 : resolved(unit) -> resolved(canonical(root(pkg/vlib/Vlib).Clock,root(/vlib?/Vlib).Clock).t)
  val direct/1 : resolved(hidden(root(pkg/impl_a/Vlib__Clock)).t)
  $ odoc compile --output-dir h --parent-id pkg/impl_a -L vlib:h/pkg/vlib -L impl_a:h/pkg/impl_a impl_a/vlib__impl_a__Extra.cmti
  $ odoc_print --short --long-paths --show-root-parents h/pkg/impl_a/vlib__impl_a__Extra.odoc
  val direct/0 : resolved(hidden(root(pkg/impl_a/Vlib__Clock)).t)

Case 5 (V1). References (R3, section 4.2).

How to read [odoc_print --refs]: each line is one reference in a doc
comment. [r(...)] is a resolved reference. [alias(p,r)] is a reference [r]
to an alias, whose target is the path [p].

[user]'s comment has [{!Vlib.Clock}] and [{!Vlib__Clock}]. R3 looks for
roots in the libraries given at link. [Vlib] is only in [vlib], so
[{!Vlib.Clock}] should mean [vlib]'s [Vlib.Clock], an alias to [vlib]'s
[Vlib__Clock]. The candidates of [{!Vlib__Clock}] are [vlib]'s unit and the
copies in the libraries given at link. A reference to a hidden unit should
also warn, and not be linked (section 4.5); odoc does neither yet
(section 11).

With both implementations given at link, there are two copies, so
[{!Vlib__Clock}] should mean [vlib]'s unit, whatever the order of the [-L]
flags. It should not be reported as ambiguous.

An "Ambiguous lookup" warning would be wrong, and so would a result that
changes with the [-L] order. The first print is of [user] as linked in
case 1.

  $ odoc_print --refs --long-paths --show-root-parents h/pkg/user/user.odocl
  r(alias(hidden(root(pkg/vlib/Vlib__Clock)),root(pkg/vlib/Vlib).Clock))
  r(hidden(root(pkg/vlib/Vlib__Clock)))
  $ odoc link -L impl_b:h/pkg/impl_b -L impl_a:h/pkg/impl_a -L vlib:h/pkg/vlib -L user:h/pkg/user h/pkg/user/user.odoc
  File "Vlib__Clock":
  Ambiguous lookup. Possible units: pkg/impl_a/Vlib__Clock, pkg/impl_b/Vlib__Clock, pkg/vlib/Vlib__Clock
  $ odoc_print --refs --long-paths --show-root-parents h/pkg/user/user.odocl
  r(alias(hidden(root(pkg/vlib/Vlib__Clock)),root(pkg/vlib/Vlib).Clock))
  r(hidden(root(pkg/vlib/Vlib__Clock)))

With only [impl_a] given at link, there is one copy, so [{!Vlib__Clock}]
should mean [impl_a]'s copy: the implementation has been chosen (R3).
[{!Vlib.Clock}] still means [vlib]'s [Vlib.Clock], as only [vlib] has a
[Vlib].

[{!Vlib__Clock}] meaning [vlib]'s unit here would be wrong, and so would an
"Ambiguous lookup" warning.

  $ odoc link -L vlib:h/pkg/vlib -L impl_a:h/pkg/impl_a -L user:h/pkg/user h/pkg/user/user.odoc
  File "Vlib__Clock":
  Ambiguous lookup. Possible units: pkg/impl_a/Vlib__Clock, pkg/vlib/Vlib__Clock
  $ odoc_print --refs --long-paths --show-root-parents h/pkg/user/user.odocl
  r(alias(hidden(root(pkg/vlib/Vlib__Clock)),root(pkg/vlib/Vlib).Clock))
  r(hidden(root(pkg/vlib/Vlib__Clock)))

Case 6 (V1). A virtual library that is not wrapped.

In a wrapped virtual library, the copy is a hidden unit, and the usual
names reach the virtual module through [vlib]'s alias, so V1's choice does
not show on a page (section 3.5). [uvlib] is a virtual library with
[(wrapped false)]: its virtual module [Timer] is the unit [Timer] itself.
[uimpl] is its one implementation. The driver documents [uvlib]'s
[timer.cmti] again in [uimpl], with [uimpl]'s [timer.ml] next to it.
[uuser] is built against [uvlib] only, and [utool] against [uimpl], and so
[uvlib]. Each has a value of type [Timer.t], and a comment with [{!Timer}].

  $ cat uvlib/timer.mli
  type t
  
  val now : unit -> t
  $ cat uuser/uuser.mli
  (** See {!Timer}. *)
  
  val now : unit -> Timer.t
  $ ocamlc -bin-annot -opaque -o uvlib/timer.cmi -c -intf uvlib/timer.mli
  $ cp uvlib/timer.cmi uimpl/
  $ ocamlc -bin-annot -opaque -I uimpl -intf-suffix .ml -o uimpl/timer.cmo -c -impl uimpl/timer.ml
  $ ocamlc -bin-annot -I uuser -I uvlib -c uuser/uuser.mli
  $ ocamlc -bin-annot -I utool -I uimpl -I uvlib -c utool/utool.mli
  $ odoc compile --output-dir h --parent-id pkg/uvlib -L uvlib:h/pkg/uvlib uvlib/timer.cmti
  $ odoc compile --output-dir h --parent-id pkg/uimpl -L uimpl:h/pkg/uimpl -L uvlib:h/pkg/uvlib uvlib/timer.cmti
  $ odoc compile-impl --output-dir h --parent-id pkg/uimpl --source-id pkg/src/uimpl/timer.ml -L uimpl:h/pkg/uimpl -L uvlib:h/pkg/uvlib uimpl/timer.cmt
  $ odoc compile --output-dir h --parent-id pkg/uuser -L uuser:h/pkg/uuser -L uvlib:h/pkg/uvlib uuser/uuser.cmti
  $ odoc compile --output-dir h --parent-id pkg/utool -L utool:h/pkg/utool -L uimpl:h/pkg/uimpl -L uvlib:h/pkg/uvlib utool/utool.cmti

[uuser]'s [now] has the origin [uuser], whose libraries hold only
[uvlib]'s [Timer], so it should mean that unit. [utool]'s [now] has the
origin [utool]. Its candidates are [uvlib]'s unit and [uimpl]'s copy. Only
[uimpl]'s library holds an implementation of [Timer], so V1 should give
[uimpl]'s copy. [utool]'s [now] meaning [uvlib]'s unit would be wrong.
odoc does not tell a copy from the virtual library's unit yet (section 11).

The references are resolved among the libraries given at link (R3). Both
links give [uimpl], the one implementation, so [{!Timer}] should mean
[uimpl]'s copy in both units. In [uuser], the path and the reference then
go to different pages (the known limit in section 4.2). [{!Timer}] meaning
[uvlib]'s unit would be wrong, and so would an "Ambiguous lookup" warning.

  $ odoc link -L uvlib:h/pkg/uvlib -L uimpl:h/pkg/uimpl -L uuser:h/pkg/uuser h/pkg/uuser/uuser.odoc
  File "Timer":
  Ambiguous lookup. Possible units: pkg/uimpl/Timer, pkg/uvlib/Timer
  $ odoc_print --short --long-paths --show-root-parents h/pkg/uuser/uuser.odocl
  val now/0 : resolved(unit) -> resolved(root(pkg/uvlib/Timer).t)
  $ odoc_print --refs --long-paths --show-root-parents h/pkg/uuser/uuser.odocl
  r(root(pkg/uvlib/Timer))
  $ odoc link -L uvlib:h/pkg/uvlib -L uimpl:h/pkg/uimpl -L utool:h/pkg/utool h/pkg/utool/utool.odoc
  File "Timer":
  Ambiguous lookup. Possible units: pkg/uimpl/Timer, pkg/uvlib/Timer
  $ odoc_print --short --long-paths --show-root-parents h/pkg/utool/utool.odocl
  val now/0 : resolved(unit) -> resolved(root(pkg/uvlib/Timer).t)
  $ odoc_print --refs --long-paths --show-root-parents h/pkg/utool/utool.odocl
  r(root(pkg/uvlib/Timer))
